+        introduction="**Skill mapping:** identify paragraph purpose; distinguish a main idea from a supporting detail; recognise paraphrase; compare scope of candidate headings.\n\n**Practice format:** matching headings. Five scored items, seven available headings. Two headings are unused.\n\n### Teaching material\n\nA heading summarises what a paragraph is doing. A paragraph may introduce a problem, explain a response, compare options or describe an unexpected result. One memorable noun is not enough to choose its heading.\n\nUse this sequence:\n\n1. Read the paragraph once without choosing. Ask: “What changes or what point is being made?”\n2. Give it a short temporary label in your own words.\n3. Read the first and last sentences again, then check the middle. A first sentence can introduce a different idea before the writer turns to the real point.\n4. Compare the scope of each heading. Reject one that covers only a small example, and reject one that adds a claim the paragraph does not make.\n5. Check the full set. Do not assign a leftover heading just because the others have been used.\n\nContrast words such as *however*, *rather than* and *instead* can signal the focus, but they are clues, not answers. A paragraph about the cost of cleaning a room is not necessarily about financing the initial building work.\n\n**Before answering:** beside each paragraph, write a three-to-seven-word label. This note is ungraded. Do not open the key until all five headings have been selected.\n\n### Original text — The room beside the old platform\n\n**A**\n\nWhen passenger trains stopped calling at Cedar Quay, the station building did not immediately lose its social role. For years, residents had met beneath its clock to exchange notices, wait for neighbours and arrange shared journeys. A café later occupied the ticket office, but a room beside the platform stayed empty. The local history group argued that the room's value was not simply its age or its wooden benches. It was one of the few places in the district that people already associated with meeting others. Finding a new use could continue that role after the railway service had ended.\n\n**B**\n\nThe first proposal was to convert the room permanently into an exhibition space. Before approving the drawings, however, the council allowed a two-week trial. Folding tables were brought in, and a paper plan of the room was pinned near the entrance. Visitors marked where they would sit, what they might bring and which activities they would prefer to do elsewhere. The organisers collected these comments before discussing any construction. Several popular suggestions had not appeared in the initial proposal. The trial was therefore a way of discovering what users wanted, rather than asking them to comment on a finished design.\n\n**C**\n\nWhat followed required fewer changes to the building than expected. The team left the internal walls in place and kept the old luggage labels above the doors. Instead of fixing a large desk to the floor, it bought small tables that could be pushed together or separated. Lightweight screens could divide a discussion group from people reading quietly, and a wheeled cupboard held supplies between sessions. Nothing in this arrangement required the room to have a single purpose. By changing the position of furniture, the volunteers could offer several uses while keeping the existing structure intact.\n\n**D**\n\nThe budget discussion initially focused on tables and lighting. Once the room began opening regularly, a different set of expenses became visible. Someone had to unlock it before each session, check it afterwards and clean the surfaces. The trial had relied on a few volunteers who happened to be free that fortnight; the permanent rota could not depend on the same arrangement. A record of these tasks showed that keeping the space available would cost more than the organisers had included in their first annual budget. They revised the plan to cover recurring work, not just the purchase of equipment.\n\n**E**\n\nFinally, the organisers had to decide which activities could happen at the same time. Some residents wanted a silent place to read, while others hoped to practise a language in pairs. Reserving the room for one group would have excluded the other. The solution was a published timetable: quiet use on several mornings, conversation sessions later in the day and a shared planning meeting once a month. The timetable was reviewed with both groups after its first month. The aim was not to select one winning activity, but to give different ways of working a predictable place in the week.\n\n### Questions 1–5\n\nChoose the correct heading **i–vii** for each paragraph. Use each selected heading once.\n\n| Heading | Text |\n| --- | --- |\n| i | Adjusting furniture instead of rebuilding |\n| ii | Recognising the cost of keeping a room open |\n| iii | Asking users to test ideas before a final decision |\n| iv | A timetable for different kinds of user |\n| v | A former meeting place with a continuing purpose |\n| vi | Funding the project by renting it to a shop |\n| vii | Replacing the old station with a new building |",
