+        title="Find the detail, then fit the answer",
+        introduction="**Skill mapping:** scan for a paraphrased detail; distinguish object/function/date/count; recover sufficient exact source words; check a grammar slot and stated answer limit.\n\n**Practice format:** sentence completion. Five scored items.\n\n### Teaching material\n\nScanning is useful only after you know what you are looking for. “What protects the objects?” and “What lists the objects?” may lead you to the same paragraph, but they require different answers.\n\nBefore scanning, identify the slot:\n\n- If an adjective is already supplied before the blank, identify the noun it describes without repeating that adjective.\n- Before *discussion cards*, a blank may need a quantity.\n- After *on a*, expect a singular countable noun phrase.\n- After *attend a*, expect an event, not a person or document.\n\nFind a likely paragraph using the question's meaning, not only a repeated word. Read the relevant sentence and at least one sentence around it. Then copy the necessary words from the text. Do not replace them with an attractive synonym when the instruction says to use words from the passage.\n\nCheck the completed sentence for meaning and grammar. Finally check the word limit. **No more than two words** is a maximum, not a requirement to use two words or copy every modifier. A shorter answer can be valid when its source words give the required meaning and fit the sentence. Do not repeat words already supplied in the question. Extra words can make the completed sentence ungrammatical or exceed the limit.\n\n**Before answering:** underline the required slot in each question. This note is ungraded. You may return to the passage as often as needed; this lesson does not impose an official exam time limit.\n\n### Original text — A museum in a travelling box\n\n**A**\n\nNorthgate Collection has a small exhibition room and a large group of schools that cannot regularly visit it. The museum's education team first tried sending photographs, but teachers reported that pupils asked questions about size, texture and how objects fitted together. The team therefore designed a travelling box. It would not contain valuable originals. Instead, it would lend carefully labelled replicas and activities that could be used in an ordinary classroom. The purpose was to support discussion, not to reproduce every part of a museum visit or replace a teacher's lesson.\n\n**B**\n\nEach box is a cardboard case with an internal frame. The replica objects sit in separate compartments divided by foam dividers. A paper inventory lists every object, while a photograph shows how the contents should be arranged before the case is closed. The packing team deliberately keeps these two documents separate: one helps a teacher check that nothing is missing, and the other makes returning the materials easier. A cloth pouch contains pieces used in a sorting activity. It is placed above the compartments rather than between the larger objects.\n\n**C**\n\nAlongside the replicas, each box contains 36 discussion cards. Some ask pupils to describe a feature before guessing its use; others encourage them to compare two possible explanations. The cards are not an answer book. At collection, the teacher checks the contents with a member of staff and records visible marks on a condition sheet. This prevents an existing mark being treated as new damage when the box returns. An inventory can confirm that an object is present, but it cannot record the condition in which it was borrowed.\n\n**D**\n\nBefore borrowing for the first time, a teacher must attend a briefing session. It lasts about half an hour and covers the packing sequence, the classroom activities and the distinction between a replica and an original object. Teachers do not have to pass a written test, and the session is not a lesson to be delivered word for word to pupils. It gives borrowers enough familiarity to use the materials confidently. A colleague can collect the case later, but the teacher responsible for the classroom work must have completed this introduction.\n\n**E**\n\nThe printed guide includes background notes and a download code. Teachers use that code to reach files containing larger versions of the photographs and printable activity sheets. The team chose not to put a memory stick in every box, because schools use different devices. The files do not contain extra questions that pupils need to finish the basic activities; those are already on the discussion cards. The digital material is an optional way to make an image easier for a class to see, not a requirement to use the box.\n\n**F**\n\nAt the end of a loan, staff compare the returned contents with the inventory and review any new marks alongside the condition sheet. They also invite a short comment from the teacher. These comments have led to small revisions, such as clearer packing photographs and a stronger pouch for the sorting pieces. The project is still a travelling collection, not a complete lesson programme. Its organisers judge success by whether the materials support worthwhile classroom questions, rather than by how many boxes leave the store in a week.\n\n### Questions 1–5\n\nComplete each sentence using **NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage**. The final sentence must be grammatical. The word limit is a maximum; a sufficient one-word answer is allowed. Do not repeat words already supplied in the question or add an explanation in the answer blank.",
