+        title="Match the feature to the right person",
+        introduction="**Skill mapping:** compare named sources/methods; track attribution across sentences; recognise paraphrase; use a repeated label when instructions allow it.\n\n**Practice format:** matching features. Five scored statements, four workshop leaders. A letter may be used more than once.\n\n### Teaching material\n\nA matching-features task can mention several people discussing the same topic. Repeated words such as *map*, *route* or *participant* may appear in every section. Match the distinctive action or view, not the shared topic.\n\nCreate a small table with three columns: *person / distinctive method / evidence*. Read each statement and identify its important relationship. “Separates waiting from walking” is more precise than “measures a journey.” Locate the passage section where that relationship is described, then read enough context to check attribution.\n\nDo not force every answer to be different. If the instructions permit repetition, two statements can describe the same person. Equally, the presence of a name near a sentence does not prove that every nearby view belongs to that person: look for reporting words and clear connections.\n\nA useful final check is to complete: “This is __ because the passage says __.” If your explanation relies on a fact about the person that you already knew, rather than this passage, reconsider the match. All workshop leaders in this lesson are fictional; no outside knowledge is required.\n\n**Before answering:** write one distinctive method beside each leader. This is an ungraded preparation note. Read all four sections before finalising answers.\n\n### Original text — Four maps of the same neighbourhood\n\nA design workshop asked four leaders to help participants represent one neighbourhood. Everyone could use the same blank outline, but the leaders disagreed about what a useful map should show. Their projects were not competing surveys of a real town. Each was a different way of examining the relationship between a route and the person describing it.\n\n**A · Mara Lin**\n\nMara began by asking long-term residents to draw a route from memory. Only afterwards did they walk it and compare their sketches with what they noticed on the ground. She was interested in places that one person included and another left out. An omission did not automatically mean a participant had made a mistake; it could show that a place played little part in that person's routine. When two residents remembered a location differently, Mara kept both accounts for discussion rather than asking the group to select a single correct memory. For her, the differences were part of the map's subject.\n\n**B · Oliver Mendez**\n\nOliver focused on the experience of getting from one place to another. Participants used a notebook to record the time spent moving and, in a separate column, time spent standing still. Waiting at a crossing or outside a busy entrance was not added to the walking total. Groups repeated a route so that one unusual delay would not dominate their account. Oliver said that a simple journey length could hide an important distinction: two routes might require similar movement, yet feel different because one included frequent pauses. His map therefore marked both the route and the places where those pauses occurred.\n\n**C · Priya Shah**\n\nPriya worked with copies of earlier published plans of the neighbourhood. She asked participants to compare what each plan chose to label, rather than to assume that the newest version explained everything. Every feature added to their final map carried the date and origin of the document from which it had been taken. If a feature appeared on only one plan, the label said so. Priya wanted readers to see how the representation had been assembled. The result was not an attempt to settle every historical question; it was a map that made the source of its information visible.\n\n**D · Niko Petrov**\n\nNiko invited people who had never visited the neighbourhood before. They noted sounds that helped them recognise where they were: a fountain, a covered walkway that changed the sound of footsteps, or a quieter stretch beside a wall. He preferred these first impressions to accounts from people who already knew where they were going. Familiar residents sometimes supplied a place name before explaining what they could actually hear. Niko's participants could not rely on those names. Their map grouped the sounds and descriptions they reported, without claiming that the map represented every visitor's experience.\n\nAt the final meeting, the leaders laid their work side by side. None proposed merging all four maps into one supposedly complete account. They asked readers to notice the question each map answered and the information it left open. A person choosing a map would first need to decide what they wanted to understand about the neighbourhood.\n\n### Questions 1–5\n\nMatch each statement with the correct leader **A–D**. **You may use any letter more than once.**\n\nA. Mara Lin\n\nB. Oliver Mendez\n\nC. Priya Shah\n\nD. Niko Petrov",
+        items=(
